\begin{proof}
Let $p$ be the smallest prime dividing $n$, and write $n=pm$. By hypothesis, $G$ has a subgroup $H$ of order $m$, so $[G:H]=p$.

Consider the action of $G$ on the left cosets of $H$. This gives a homomorphism $\varphi:G\to S_p$. If $G$ were simple, then $\ker\varphi$ would be either trivial or all of $G$. It cannot be all of $G$, since the coset action is nontrivial. Hence $\varphi$ is injective, and therefore $n=|G|$ divides $p!$.

But $p$ is the smallest prime dividing $n$, so every prime divisor of $n$ is at least $p$. Since $n\mid p!$, every prime divisor of $n$ is at most $p$. Thus $p$ is the only prime divisor of $n$. Moreover, $p!$ contains exactly one factor of $p$, so $n\mid p!$ implies $n=p$, contradicting that $n$ is composite. Therefore $G$ is not simple.
\end{proof}

